{{nihongo|'''Anjō Motonobu'''|安条元信}} (7 December 1534 – 3 October 1589) was a [[daimyō]] of the [[Sengoku Period]] in [[Mitoyagi]]. He served as the leader of the Anjō clan from 1564 until his death at the [[Battle of Saikawa]]. Regarded as one of the most reliable and trusted advisors to [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]], Motonobu played a central role in the unification of Okaiken. His daughter, [[Hatsu-no-kata]] (also known as Hatsune-no-kata), became the primary wife of Shigetada, cementing the Anjō clan's status as the most prominent vassals of the emerging Imperial line.

Motonobu is celebrated in Sanese history for his mastery of marine and riverine warfare, his legendary physical strength, and his role in establishing the [[Anjō-Murakami Suigun]], which formed the backbone of the first national navy of Mitoyagi. His tactical innovations, such as forgoing heavy armor for combat mobility and the use of flat-bottomed transport vessels, left a lasting mark on the military and cultural identity of the nation.

==Early life==
[[File:Mikata_River_Landscape.png|thumb|right|250px|The river on the outskirts of Mikata where Motonobu spent his youth.]]
Motonobu was born in [[Miyagi province]], the son of {{nihongo|Anjō Motonaga|安条元長}}. His father was a loyal administrator for the [[Taketoyo clan]], the lords of [[Kyoshu]]. Motonobu spent his early childhood in a small village on the outskirts of [[Mikata]], surrounded by rice paddies and lush green mountains. Despite his samurai lineage, he spent much of his childhood outdoors, often found playing by the river that ran through the village or chasing fish, an experience that historians believe formed his lifelong affinity for aquatic environments and swimming mastery.

As he grew older, the family moved into a larger home in the town of Mikata to accommodate his father's administrative duties. Mikata was a bustling center for traders and merchants from all over Okaiken, and Motonobu was fascinated by the sights and sounds of the marketplaces. In his early teens, he began to receive formal samurai training in the sword and bow from his father and other clan members. However, his true passion was building raw physical strength. He was known for his love of lifting heavy objects, often challenging other youths to contests of strength involving rocks, bundles of rice, or logs. This training was fueled by a voracious appetite; it was common for Motonobu to eat double the meal of a normal retainer, much to the exasperation of the clan's cooks. These habits contributed to his powerful appearance, setting him apart from his peers even at a young age.

Despite his rigorous training, Motonobu never lost his love for the outdoors. He continued to spend much of his free time exploring the surrounding countryside, where he would hike up into the mountains or paddle down the local rivers. It was during one of these excursions that he first encountered the neighboring province of Sanno and its powerful daimyō, {{nihongo|[[Ogasawara Terumasa]]|小笠原照正}}, who would ultimately play a pivotal role in Motonobu's later life.

==Leader of the Anjō clan==
Motonobu assumed leadership of the clan on 12 October 1564, at the age of 19, following the sudden and violent murder of his father. Motonaga, while serving as a loyal administrator for the Taketoyo clan, was killed by an angry mob of peasants and local merchants. The mob was protesting the oppressive policies and tax levies implemented by their lord, {{nihongo|[[Taketoyo Hideyasu]]|武豊秀安}}, and directed their fury at Motonaga as the clan's local representative. 

Directed at Motonaga as the local figurehead, the mob marched to the Anjō residence intending to kill the son as well. When the 19-year-old Motonobu emerged to confront them, his imposing physical presence and furious demeanor caused the crowd to halt. Their courage failed them, and they dispersed soon after, leaving Motonobu in charge of a clan whose relationship with their Taketoyo masters had been irrevocably severed by violence.

[[File:Anjo_Motosada_Portrait.png|thumb|left|200px|A contemporary sketch of Anjō Motosada, Motonobu's uncle and political rival during the succession crisis.]]
Following the dispersal of the mob, the Anjō clan fell into an immediate succession crisis. Although Motonobu was the direct heir, his young age and his unconventional, brutish reputation made him a highly controversial choice among the clan elders. A powerful conservative faction quickly formed around his uncle, {{nihongo|[[Anjō Motosada]]|安条元貞}}, who served as the chief financial overseer of Mikata. Motosada viewed the peasant uprising not just as a tragedy, but as a direct rebellion against the Taketoyo clan that threatened to bring the wrath of Hideyasu down upon their entire family.

Motosada argued that the Anjō must immediately march on the peasant leaders, execute them, and send their heads—along with Motonobu as a temporary hostage—to Taketoyo Hideyasu to prove their unwavering loyalty and avoid total annihilation by Taketoyo reprisals. Motonobu, grieving and enraged, vehemently opposed this appeasement strategy. He argued that the Taketoyo's oppressive taxation had directly caused his father's death and that punishing the commoners of Mikata would only ignite a full-scale regional rebellion that the weakened Anjō could not survive. 

[[File:Anjo_Motonobu aged 25.png|thumb|right|200px|A depiction of Motonobu aged 25.]]

The intra-family dispute climaxed during an emergency clan council held just two days after Motonaga's funeral. Historical accounts from the era state that the debate grew violently heated. When Motosada attempted to physically bar Motonobu from taking the seat of the clan head, asserting that a boy could not navigate the coming political storm, the teenage Motonobu seized the heavy oak table separating them and threw it across the room with a single arm. The terrifying display of raw physical power, coupled with a passionate speech accusing the elders of cowardice and betrayal of his father's memory, shattered the conservative opposition. Motosada was immediately placed under house arrest, and his key supporters were stripped of their administrative ranks within the hour. By late October 1564, Motonobu had successfully purged his internal rivals and consolidated absolute control over the Anjō clan, but his refusal to enact brutal reprisals against the merchants and peasants of Mikata sowed the seeds of deep suspicion within the Taketoyo leadership.

==Switching Allegiances==
[[File:Hachisuka_Koroku_Advisor.png|thumb|left|200px|Portrait of Hachisuka Koroku, the Taketoyo advisor who orchestrated the Anjō clan's demotion.]]
For two years, Motonobu ruled Mikata with simmering resentment toward the Taketoyo. He eventually discovered that the mob which murdered his father had been secretly agitated and funded by {{nihongo|[[Hachisuka Koroku]]|蜂須賀小六}}, a trusted Taketoyo advisor. Koroku, seeking to gain control of the strategic town of Mikata for himself, had orchestrated the unrest to remove the Anjō clan from power. In 1566, Koroku's maneuvers succeeded: he convinced Lord Taketoyo Hideyasu that the Anjō clan could not be trusted, and Motonobu was officially demoted from a fudai (hereditary vassal) to a tozama (outsider) daimyō. This disenfranchisement included being stripped of influence and banned from clan meetings, which Motonobu viewed as a final insult.

The mistreatment of the Anjō clan rapidly escalated under the subsequent administration of {{nihongo|[[Taketoyo Sadahiro]]|武豊貞広}}. Sadahiro viewed the demoted Anjō with open contempt, levying crippling, retaliatory taxes on the merchants of Mikata to fund his failing military campaigns and allowing Koroku to systematically dismantle the Anjō's remaining territorial rights. Realizing that his clan faced total eradication if they remained subservient, Motonobu secretly reached out to the Taketoyo's primary rival, Ogasawara Terumasa, pledging his allegiance. In a desperate bid for freedom, Motonobu led an open revolt inside the city of Mikata to seize total control from the Taketoyo garrisons. Although the revolt was suppressed and Motonobu was arrested, Terumasa—seeing an opportunity to gain a strategic foothold and a capable naval commander—pressured the Taketoyo for his release. Facing mounting military pressure from the Ogasawara, Taketoyo Sadahiro relented, allowing Motonobu to flee Mikata.

Following his liberation, Motonobu formally entered Terumasa's service. To solidify this new alliance and ensure the survival of his clan, Motonobu offered his daughter, Hatsu-no-kata, to be wed to Ogasawara Terumasa. However, Terumasa, who viewed the newly defected Anjō clan as useful tools but socially beneath his own prestige, curtly refused the marriage offer. This rejection threatened to severely humiliate Motonobu and leave the Anjō politically isolated within the Ogasawara power structure.

It was at this critical juncture that Honda Shigetada, then a rising general under Terumasa, intervened. Recognizing Motonobu's strategic value and seeking to preserve the dignity of the Anjō clan, Shigetada unexpectedly offered to take Hatsu-no-kata as his own wife. Motonobu was profoundly moved by this honorable act, which saved his family from disgrace and secured their political position. In response, Motonobu pledged his absolute, unwavering loyalty to Shigetada, deciding to become his direct vassal.

==Service under Honda Shigetada==
In July 1579, while Honda Shigetada was laying siege to the castle town of Nakaoka in the neighboring province of [[Kōchi]], he met Motonobu in the town of Mikata. Seeking to ensure his clan's survival after defecting from the Taketoyo, Motonobu intended to send his daughter, Hatsu-no-kata, to be wed to Ogasawara Terumasa. When Terumasa declined the offer, Shigetada unexpectedly offered to become her husband himself. Motonobu, moved by the act, decided to become a vassal of Shigetada. This union transformed the Anjō into the primary vassals of the Honda clan, where they eventually took various high-level government positions in the bakufu. On 4 October 1580, their first child, Nabenosuke (later the Prince Takahito, then [[Emperor Daigo]]), was born, securing the future of the lineage.

In June 1581, news arrived that Shigetada's adoptive father, {{nihongo|[[Honda Shigetatsu]]|本多重辰}}, was terminally ill and wished for Shigetada to lead the Honda clan. Terumasa approved the move on 8 August 1581, and Shigetada officially adopted the name {{nihongo|Heihachirō|平八郎}}, a name passed down to first-born sons or successors for generations. Following Shigetatsu's death on 18 November 1581, Shigetada became the head of the Honda clan and moved the headquarters to [[Suemori]]. Motonobu played a vital role during this transition, as Terumasa allowed Shigetada to move the clan with a degree of independence not seen with other Ogasawara retainers, allowing Motonobu to consolidate the naval power of the Suemori domain.

[[File:Honda_Shigetada_Motonobu_Council.png|thumb|right|250px|A Honda-period depiction of Anjō Motonobu advising Honda Shigetada at Suemori Castle.]]
The familial ties between the two men deepened significantly with the birth of Shigetada and Hatsu-no-kata's second son, [[Honda Shigetomo]] (childhood name Naiki), in 1582, followed closely by a third son, [[Honda Shigetoki]] (Naimon), born to a concubine. By serving as the father-in-law to the future Emperor and grandfather to a future [[Shogun]], Motonobu secured an untouchable position as Shigetada's most trusted advisor. Motonobu quickly distinguished himself not just as a family patriarch, but as a supremely capable military commander. He was formally recognized as one of the {{nihongo|Four Honda Heavenly Pillars|本多四天王}}, serving alongside other legendary figures like [[Ii Naomasa]] and [[Sakakibara Yasumasa]]. Because of this immense trust, Motonobu was frequently entrusted with the command of the clan's main armies. While Shigetada was famously known across Okaiken as "The Warrior who surpassed Death itself"—having never sustained a significant wound in over forty battles—contemporary historians attribute much of this invulnerability to Motonobu's impenetrable vanguard defenses and flawless logistical support.

[[File:Battle_of_Uomura_Duel.png|thumb|left|250px|A historical illustration showing the Battle of Uomura, where Motonobu secured the perimeter for Shigetada's famous duel.]]
Motonobu frequently fought directly by Shigetada's side or commanded independent theaters. On 14 July 1582, he supported Shigetada at the [[Battle of Uomura]], securing the battlefield perimeter while Shigetada famously defeated [[Yamanaka Shikanosuke]] in a one-on-one duel. Shortly after, Motonobu led a naval bombardment during the [[Siege of Wakamatsu]] in [[Kaga Province]], helping to suppress the remnants of the minor [[Hachisuka clan]].

As the Honda clan's power grew throughout the 1580s, Motonobu's responsibilities expanded, and he played a crucial, vengeful role in bringing about the downfall of his former masters, the Taketoyo. In April 1584, tasked by Ogasawara Terumasa to take advantage of the weakening Taketoyo clan, Shigetada ordered Motonobu to lay siege to the Taketoyo stronghold at [[Temmokuzan]]. The successful siege culminated in the seppuku of [[Taketoyo Sadayori]], effectively destroying the once-powerful clan and expanding Honda control over [[Musashino Province]].

[[File:Retreat_from_Yunami.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A 19th-century painting depicting the strategic retreat of the Honda forces following the Battle of Yunami.]]
His status as a primary commander was further highlighted when Shigetada gave him independent control of the Honda forces during the [[Battle of Yunami]] on 14 December 1585. Facing the powerful [[Yamagata clan]], Motonobu's forces were ultimately defeated by the brilliant tactician [[Yamagata Masakage]] and forced into a strategic retreat to [[Nagashino Castle|Nagashino]]. Following the defeat at Yunami and subsequent territorial losses to the Uesugi in early 1586, a furious Ogasawara Terumasa recalled Shigetada to Nagaya castle and ordered him to commit seppuku for his failures. It was Motonobu, alongside a coalition of senior Honda vassals, who vehemently intervened. Motonobu argued forcefully to Terumasa that Shigetada's death would leave the Ogasawara completely vulnerable to an Uesugi invasion from the north. The successful petition saved Shigetada's life, cementing an unbreakable bond of absolute loyalty between the two men that lasted until Motonobu's tragic death.

==Military Campaigns==
===Battle of the Reeds===
[[File:Motonobu_Flatbottom_Boat.png|thumb|right|250px|Diagram of the flat-bottomed transport vessels utilized by Motonobu during the Invasion of Musashino.]]
During the [[Invasion of Musashino]], Motonobu served as the premier naval commander for the Honda forces. The geography of the Musashi region presented a daunting challenge: a vast, interconnected network of deep, muddy rivers and marshlands that rendered the formidable Honda cavalry effectively useless. The Taketoyo loyalists, commanded by veteran defenders, had heavily fortified all primary stone bridges and major fords, anticipating a straightforward, grinding assault. Faced with the heavily fortified river defenses of the Musashi region, he devised a strategy using flat-bottomed transport vessels, known as ''kawabune'', to bypass primary land routes entirely. Motonobu oversaw the rapid construction of over two hundred of these shallow-draft boats, utilizing green timber to speed up production. 

Under the cover of a moonless autumn night, Motonobu orchestrated a silent amphibious assault directly through the dense, seemingly impassable reed beds. As the vanguard neared the shore, the enemy was alerted, and sudden arquebus fire rained down from the embankments. The thick mud caused the lead boats to become firmly stuck, threatening to stall the landing and turn it into a massacre. Personally leading the amphibious assault, Motonobu was observed jumping into waist-deep water to pull landing craft onto the shore by hand. Plunging into freezing mud that reached his chest, and disregarding the incoming fire, his massive frame acted as a human towline, roaring orders that galvanized his men. Inspired by his raw display of courage, the Honda infantry surged forward, overwhelming the stunned defenders and collapsing the defensive line. This victory secured the eastern supply routes for Shigetada and established Motonobu as the premier naval commander of the era.

===Battle of Morihama===
[[File:Motonobu_Log_Lift_Battle.png|thumb|left|250px|An Edo-period woodblock print illustrating Motonobu lifting the massive log at the Battle of Morihama.]]
Motonobu's reputation for raw power was solidified at the [[Battle of Morihama]]. Following the breakthrough at the river networks, the Honda vanguard pushed rapidly inland, only to be stalled at the steep Morihama Gorge, a treacherous natural chasm. When a vital bridge was destroyed by retreating enemy forces, stalling the Honda advance, the vanguard found themselves trapped and highly vulnerable to volley fire from archers positioned on the opposite cliff. Commanding the center army, Motonobu arrived to find his troops pinned down and morale plummeting. Assessing the dire situation, he noticed a massive, recently felled pine tree—estimated by later chroniclers to weigh well over five hundred kilograms—near the edge of the chasm. 

Stripping off his upper armor and throwing aside his weapons, Motonobu reportedly lifted a massive fallen log single-handedly to create a temporary crossing. With agonizing effort, he hoisted the enormous trunk onto his bare shoulders, carried it to the edge, and forcefully slammed it across the gap, the wood splintering against the opposite stone bank. He then stood firm at the base of the log, acting as a human anchor to keep the timber steady while the elite infantry carefully crossed single file. This incredible feat allowed the cavalry to eventually charge and shatter the enemy's rear guard, turning a potential rout into a decisive victory. This event is the historical inspiration for the modern [[Goriki]] competition's Motonobu Log Lift.

===Invasion of Yagikoshu===
As the Head of the Left Army during the massive invasion of [[Yagikoshu]], Motonobu secured the turbulent northern straits against [[Uesugi clan|Uesugi]]-aligned pirate fleets, specifically the notorious Kotsuna corsairs. Recognizing that inland river tactics would not suffice on the open ocean, he consolidated various rival maritime families into a unified, disciplined force. He was instrumental in building several ships that would become the backbone of the first navy of Mitoyagi, the Anjō-Murakami Suigun. He introduced standardized naval doctrines, replacing chaotic boarding skirmishes with disciplined formation sailing. 

Under his direct supervision, shipyards constructed fleets of heavily armed ''sekibune'' (medium warships) equipped with rudimentary naval artillery, thick bamboo shielding, and anti-boarding netting. Motonobu instituted a decentralized fleet structure, allowing smaller, highly maneuverable squadrons to constantly harass Uesugi supply convoys while his main galleons held the strategic straits. Under his command, the Left Army maintained a continuous blockade that crippled the northern defenders' ability to receive reinforcements from the mainland, effectively starving out several coastal fortresses before the infantry even made landfall.

===Battle of Tsudanouchi===
{{main|Battle of Tsudanouchi}}
In the [[Tsuitsui region]], Motonobu attempted to break complex enemy defensive lines through a highly coordinated land and sea assault, intended to be the climax of his early Yagikoshu campaign. The strategy relied on heavy naval bombardment from the Suigun to soften the coastal forts using newly acquired foreign culverins, followed immediately by a swift infantry push. While initial progress was made at [[Tsuruguchi]], disrupting enemy supply lines and allowing Honda forces to push deep into the interior, the tactical situation quickly deteriorated. 

The [[Ino clan]], led by cunning local tacticians who recognized that Motonobu's forces had overextended themselves from their naval support, launched a fierce counterattack to the west of Tsudanouchi from the forested hills. Simultaneously, an unexpectedly high spring tide followed by a rapid ebb trapped Motonobu's supporting fleet in the muddy shallows, neutralizing his naval artillery and leaving the infantry without cover fire. Facing severe troop exhaustion, treacherous muddy terrain, and rising tides, Motonobu made the difficult decision on 2 September 1589 to order a tactical retreat. He personally commanded the rearguard, wielding a heavy iron kanabō to smash through the advancing spearmen, fighting a bloody withdrawal that held off the Ino vanguard just long enough. This desperate maneuver preserved the core of the Left Army, though it left Motonobu isolated and highly vulnerable as he retreated toward the fortress of [[Mogami]].

==Death==
{{main|Battle of Saikawa}}

After being defeated at the Battle of Tsudanouchi on 2 September 1589, Motonobu retreated to [[Mogami Castle]] on 4 September 1589. There, he had asked Shigetada through messengers to send more troops for the left army by five weeks or risk losing the entire left army. Mogami castle lay in a floodplain and the monsoon season had just begun. Motonobu had mastered water battles from an offensive standpoint but had never in defense. On 19 September 1589, Shigetada replied back through another messenger, writing that his own forces were in trouble from the Uesugi forces and suggested Motonobu to retreat 40 miles to [[Fukuda Castle]]. However, the messenger was intercepted by the Uesugi forces and was killed. {{nihongo|[[Shigekawa Toshiyuki]]|重川利幸}}, a retainer of the Uesugi, would use the intercepted intel and instructions against Motonobu's forces. Motonobu, anticipating his death after never receiving Shigetada's orders, wrote a death poem to Shigetada as a response; saying that it would be the last time they would hear from each other. It read like a suicide note once Shigetada receives it.

"My name shall cross several seas and reach the realms of the ''Hokuban''," the letter's epilogue read.

On 30 September 1589, while Motonobu and what was left of the Left army was preparing to cross the [[Sai River]] to retreat at Fukuda Castle, the Uesugi forces attacked from three sides. Some of the Left army regiments were able to return to Mogami castle but Motonobu was trapped in the middle of the river with the current steadily increasing from moderate rain in the morning, intensifying by the time of the skirmish. With the rain heavily pouring on the battlefield, Motonobu was unable to proceed with his horse and dismounted. He tried to tread his way back to Mogami castle but was encountered by two Uesugi soldiers who had pursued after him. The two soldiers were able to mortally wound Motonobu in a melee—inflicting a deep shoulder wound and another cut to the chest—but were unable to kill him as he was swept away by the river's strengthening current when he fell.

Motonobu was carried away by the current for five kilometres but miraculously never sank underwater during the ordeal; eventually Motonobu ended up at the shores of [[Kozuke-Sai]], an outlying village that supports Mogami castle. His survival, which would have been impossible for any other soldier, was attributed to a unique combination of factors. His decision to forgo heavy armour like a ''[[kabuto]]'' (helmet) and ''[[sode (armour)|sode]]'' (shoulder guards) prevented him from being immediately dragged underwater. Furthermore, his immense physical strength and lifelong mastery of swimming, honed since childhood in the rivers of Mikata, allowed him to stay afloat despite his grievous wounds. The icy cold of the monsoon-swollen river also played a crucial role, causing severe vasoconstriction that slowed his bleeding and kept him from bleeding out during the 5&nbsp;km journey. He was later found by villagers, still conscious but bleeding heavily and in the advanced stages of shock.

[[File:Saikawa AI Generated.png|350px|thumb|right|A modern interpretation of the Battle of Saikawa, where Motonobu was killed.]]

The villagers were able to move and hide Motonobu at one of the storehouses in the village. Motonobu, beginning to lose consciousness, mentioned to the villagers that he had failed his clan and Honda Shigetada. The villagers comforted Motonobu by providing bandages and tea but Motonobu temporarily lost his consciousness due to shock. On 2 October 1589 he had regained his consciousness and begged for a villager to bring him a dagger so he can commit ''[[seppuku]]'' as he had lost his weapons in the river when he was swept away by the current. Much to Motonobu's disappointment, the villager was unable to bring the dagger as he was just a lowly merchant and none of the farmers are willing to give away their tools for a futile act, in addition, none of them have swords to behead him to complete the process of ''seppuku''. The villagers instead comforted Motonobu by serving him more tea, fed him food and were able to stop external bleeding of Motonobu's wounds. However, the villagers realized that Motonobu had lost a considerable amount of blood when he was carried away by the river and was bleeding internally.

[[File:Motonobu dying.png|350px|thumb|right|A modern interpretation of the aftermath of the battle, where Motonobu dies.]]

One of the merchants asked Motonobu if he had any requests for Honda Shigetada. Motonobu declined as he already sent him a letter telling him that he was going to die before venturing out of Mogami castle, and went on to say a word of gratitude to the merchant who did not abandon him even though the territory had returned to the Uesugi's control. He requested to have his body buried in the village if he dies as it was the first and last time that ordinary citizens had helped him. He then gave the villager one of his buddha amulets that he had been carrying, before Motonobu lost consciousness a final time. Motonobu died on 3 October 1589 at the age of 44 years old.

Days later, the village of Kozuke-Sai handed over Motonobu's body and his armour to Honda Shigetada, who was moved to tears knowing that he just lost one of his most important retainers of the clan. As what Motonobu had wished to the villagers, Shigetada promised that he would come to the village one day and bury Motonobu at [[Enkōji temple|Enkōji]], the only temple of the village. Motonobu's body was cremated at Mikata, and his bones and ashes would be sent to Enkōji after the Uesugi were driven out by Shigetada in the Second Invasion of Yagikoshu. 

The remaining regiments of the Left army, under {{nihongo|[[Sakakibara Yasuhide]]|榊原康秀}}, retreated back to [[Kii castle]] on 6 October 1589, where he was able to sign a non-aggression pact with the Uesugi and allow them to return to [[Haruguni]] without harm.

==Appearance==
[[File:Photo03.png |250px|thumb|right|Anjō Motonobu's ''Shōjō Jinbaori'', on display at the National Museum of History, Okami, Okaiken.]]
Western accounts describe Motonobu as standing between 189 and 190 centimeters tall, possessing a large, muscular physical structure that dwarfed other retainers and lords around him, including Honda Shigetada. He is often referred to as oni-Motonobu due to his size and physical strength. Motonobu usually had long unkempt hair, depicted as dark brown and flowing past his shoulders, occasionally tied at the back. He also grew a full beard and a moustache.

His choice of armor was highly unconventional, as he omitted the [[sode (armour)|sode]] (shoulder armor) to ensure maximum flexibility for his arms in combat, particularly while steering boats or wielding weapons in close quarters. This style was later adopted by Shigetada's son [[Honda Shigetomo]]. He eschewed the [[kabuto]] for a simple [[hachigane]] (forehead protector), consistently shown as a dark, simple band of cloth or leather tied around his head. His signature garment was a dark red shōjō jinbaori made from [[Peilanese]] and [[Kwangju]] materials. Formal portraits may show this vest with an ornate gold trim, while battlefield depictions show a practical, plain red version. The original vest, found ripped after the Battle of Saikawa, is preserved at the [[National Museum of History]] in [[Okami]].

==Legacy==
Motonobu's primary legacy is the establishment of the maritime military tradition of Mitoyagi. The Anjō-Murakami Suigun, which he structured with strict naval doctrines, standardized ship designs, and decentralized squadron tactics, completely revolutionized oceanic warfare in the region. This force directly evolved into the [[Imperial Mitoyagi Navy]] following the establishment of the Honda Shogunate and serves as the institutional ancestor to the modern [[Sanese Naval Defense Forces]] and later the [[Sanese Imperial Navy]]. His family line remained remarkably influential in the centuries that followed; his son [[Anjō Motoyasu]] succeeded him as Lord of Mikata, and the Anjō clan maintained a near-monopoly on high-ranking naval administration positions within the Shogunate. Furthermore, [[Hatsu-no-kata|Hatsu-no-kata's]] marriage to Shigetada ensured the clan's permanent place within the highest echelons of the new imperial aristocracy. In recognition of his immense sacrifice, Shigetada ordered the renaming of the town of Mogami—where the disastrous retreat took place—to Anjō. This name persists today in the modern city of [[Anjō-Saikawa]], serving as a geographical monument to his loyalty.

Furthermore, Motonobu's appearance left a profound and lasting mark on the cultural consciousness of Okaiken. Often compared in contemporary texts to a "living [[Niō]]" (a wrathful, muscle-bound Baiduist guardian deity) due to his immense bulk and his habit of fighting with exposed limbs, Motonobu became the historical archetype for the ideal male physique in Mitoyagi culture. Unlike neighboring continental cultures that often prioritized refined, scholarly, or slender aesthetics for their nobility, in Okaiken, physical density and raw, functional strength became intrinsically synonymous with absolute loyalty, guardianship, and the sacred ability to protect the Emperor. This philosophical link between muscle and morality—often termed the "Motonobu Ideal"—persists deeply in modern Okaiken. It influenced early imperial military training standards and is a major reason why bodybuilding and rigorous physical conditioning are highly revered national pastimes today, where the "guardian physique" remains the ultimate expression of masculinity.

===Goriki===
{{further|Goriki}}
[[File:Goriki_Kure_Classic.png|thumb|right|250px|A [[Goriki]] competitor performing the Motonobu Log Lift at the Kure Heavy Industries Strongman Classic.]]
Tying directly into the modern [[Forge State]] ideology and the national obsession with physical power, {{nihongo|[[Goriki]]|強力}} is Okaiken's unique, localized version of strongman competitions. The sport traces its cultural and spiritual lineage directly back to Sengoku-era warriors like Motonobu, who were celebrated not merely for their martial skill, but for their functional, battlefield-altering strength. Modern Goriki events intentionally eschew traditional gym weights in favor of objects tied to the nation's industrial and naval history. 

Events at the premier Kure Heavy Industries Strongman Classic include dragging multi-ton ship anchors, loading heavy industrial steel cogs onto platforms, and the traditional lifting of massive spherical shrine stones. However, the climax of every major tournament is the [[Motonobu Log Lift]]. This grueling overhead press event serves as a direct, physical homage to Motonobu's legendary feat of strength at the Battle of Morihama where he lifted a massive pine log single-handedly to bridge a gorge. In modern competitions, athletes are required to clean and press a specially crafted, unbalanced timber replica weighing upwards of 180 kilograms. The event is a staple of national sports broadcasting in Okaiken, ensuring that the visual legacy of the "Demon Admiral" is renewed for every generation.

==In Popular Culture==
[[File:Motonobu_Statue_Mikata.png|thumb|left|A statue of Anjō Motonobu in his birthplace of Mikata, Miyagi.]]
Motonobu has been a recurring figure in Sanese media, most notably in the 60th [[Taisen Drama]], Oni-Motonobu (2021), where he was portrayed by {{nihongo|Takayama Kenta|高山健太}}. He also appears as a prominent supporting character in the 65th anniversary drama, Hatsu: Empress of the Tides (2026). His muscular physique became the archetype for the ideal male body in Okaiken culture, fueling the national passion for physical conditioning and the "guardian physique" aesthetic.

